<section class="section"><div class="shell">
  <div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">What It Answers</div><h2>Where Is Search Leverage Hiding?</h2></div><p class="section-intro">A useful search strategy starts with evidence. Gwap looks at what can be observed publicly, then incorporates Search Console or other first-party data when you choose to provide it.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card"><small>01 // Visibility</small><h3>What can search engines actually see?</h3><p>We examine discoverability, indexability signals, page coverage, internal linking, metadata, and obvious technical friction that can suppress visibility.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><small>02 // Demand</small><h3>What are people already looking for?</h3><p>Search behavior is translated into customer intent, recurring problems, questions, comparisons, and commercial themes worth pursuing.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><small>03 // Gaps</small><h3>Where are you absent?</h3><p>We identify missing pages, weak topic coverage, unclear search intent, and places where competitors or larger publishers are capturing attention you could compete for.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><small>04 // Recovery</small><h3>What needs fixing before more content?</h3><p>Indexing, canonical, crawl, sitemap, internal-link, and page-quality problems are separated from content opportunities so effort goes to the correct layer first.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><small>05 // Opportunity</small><h3>Which pages deserve to exist next?</h3><p>We prioritize search opportunities by intent, business relevance, competitive difficulty, evidence, and the likelihood that the topic can support a useful customer journey.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><small>06 // Action</small><h3>What should happen first?</h3><p>You receive a sequenced action plan instead of a generic SEO checklist, with clear distinctions between fixes, experiments, content, and longer-term bets.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="section"><div class="shell split">
  <div>
    <div class="eyebrow">Your Search Intelligence File</div>
    <h2>Evidence → Priority → Move.</h2>
    <ul class="list">
      <li><strong>Search visibility baseline</strong> — what is visible, missing, weak, or blocked.</li>
      <li><strong>Indexing and discovery review</strong> — sitemap, crawl, canonical, internal-link, and page-level friction.</li>
      <li><strong>Demand and intent map</strong> — the problems, questions, and commercial themes customers are searching around.</li>
      <li><strong>Content gap analysis</strong> — where your current site fails to answer demand you could credibly serve.</li>
      <li><strong>Priority opportunity map</strong> — what deserves action now, later, or not at all.</li>
      <li><strong>Search action plan</strong> — a sequenced plan for fixes, pages, experiments, and measurement.</li>
      <li><strong>Measurement framework</strong> — what to watch as pages move from discovery to impressions, clicks, leads, and business outcomes.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="scope">
    <div class="eyebrow">Pilot Scope</div>
    <h3>Search intelligence without SEO theater.</h3>
    <p><strong>Input:</strong> your website, business objective, target customer, and the search problem you want investigated.</p>
    <p><strong>Optional:</strong> Search Console exports or first-party performance data can deepen the analysis, but are not required to begin.</p>
    <p><strong>Included:</strong> research, diagnosis, prioritization, opportunity mapping, and an execution plan.</p>
    <p><strong>Not included:</strong> guaranteed rankings, backlink schemes, mass AI content, paid media spend, or unlimited implementation.</p>
    <p><strong>Commercial status:</strong> V0.1 pilot. Scope and delivery are confirmed before work begins. A dedicated checkout will be added after pilot pricing is finalized.</p>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="section"><div class="shell">
  <div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">Method</div><h2>Search Is A Business Signal.</h2></div><p class="section-intro">Gwap treats search as a demand and distribution system, not a pile of keywords. The work connects technical visibility, customer intent, content, and conversion.</p></div>
  <div class="process">
    <div class="step"><small>01 // Observe</small><h3>Map The Current Surface</h3><p>Pages, indexing signals, search presence, site structure, and visible friction.</p></div>
    <div class="step"><small>02 // Interpret</small><h3>Connect Demand To Intent</h3><p>Separate curiosity from research, comparison, commercial intent, and high-value customer problems.</p></div>
    <div class="step"><small>03 // Prioritize</small><h3>Choose The Opportunity</h3><p>Rank fixes and opportunities by evidence, business fit, difficulty, dependency, and expected usefulness.</p></div>
    <div class="step"><small>04 // Execute</small><h3>Build The Search Path</h3><p>Fix discovery first, then publish the pages and content that deserve to exist, then measure what happens.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="section"><div class="shell split">
  <div><div class="eyebrow">Best Fit</div><h2>For Sites With Something Worth Finding.</h2></div>
  <div><ul class="list">
    <li>You already have a website, offer, publication, or operating business.</li>
    <li>You know organic visibility could be stronger but do not know whether the problem is technical, editorial, competitive, or strategic.</li>
    <li>You want to understand demand before producing another pile of content.</li>
    <li>You need priorities tied to business outcomes, not vanity ranking screenshots.</li>
    <li>You want a bounded intelligence layer before committing to ongoing SEO work.</li>
  </ul></div>
</div></section>

<section class="section"><div class="shell">
  <div class="boundary"><div class="boundary-grid">
    <div><div class="eyebrow">Gwap Standard</div><h3>No Ranking Promises.</h3></div>
    <div><p>Search results are controlled by search engines and competitive markets. Gwap does not guarantee rankings, traffic, leads, or revenue from a specific page.</p><p>Observed facts, estimates, hypotheses, and recommendations are kept distinct. When first-party data is unavailable, the analysis says so rather than pretending public evidence can answer everything.</p></div>
